prompt:
A 12-line method is refactored into nine private one-line methods, most of which are called from exactly one place:

```java
void processOrder(Order order) {
    validate(order);
    applyDiscount(order);
    reserveInventory(order);
    chargePayment(order);
    notifyCustomer(order);
}

private void validate(Order order) { checkNotNull(order); }
private void checkNotNull(Order order) {
    if (order == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
}
// ... four more single-line, single-use private methods follow the same pattern
```

One reviewer says: "This is cleaner now -- every method is tiny." A second reviewer disagrees, arguing that following the logic now requires jumping between many one-line, single-use methods with no reuse benefit. Which position best reflects the readability/maintainability trade-off at play?
